{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And one who curses his father.</b> I have only [the case of] while alive; from where [do I derive] after death? Scripture says, 'and one who curses' — in any case. We have heard the punishment; from where the warning? Scripture says: 'Elohim you shall not curse' (Exod. 22:27). 'And a prince among your people you shall not execrate.' 'You shall not curse the deaf' (Exod. 22:27). The common denominator among them — in all three — is that they are 'among your people'; you are warned concerning cursing them. So too your father and your mother, who are among your people — you are warned concerning cursing them. \\n\\nWhy he separated between 'one who strikes' and 'one who curses' with the verse 'and one who steals a man' — because most of those who are stolen, when they are young and grow up in a foreign land, do not recognize their parents, and through a quarrel the matter leads to striking them and cursing them. Therefore he placed between them 'and one who steals a man and sells him.' And he juxtaposed 'and one who curses' to it, because of the quarrel, in order to juxtapose quarrel to quarrel. \\n\\nAnd one cannot say: let the Merciful One write 'one who curses' and all the more so 'one who strikes,' for we do not punish from an a fortiori inference. And furthermore, their deaths are not equal — 'one who strikes' is by strangulation, and 'one who curses' is by stoning. And what we find, that one who strikes his father is punished by the lighter [death, namely] strangulation, and one who curses [is punished] by stoning — is because cursing applies even to the exalted rank and honor of father and mother, it was likened to the honor of HaShem; but striking does not apply to HaShem. As our Rabbis taught in Kiddushin: it says 'one who curses his father and his mother shall surely be put to death' (Exod. 21:17), and it says 'any man who curses his Elohim etc.' (Lev. 24:15) — Scripture equated the cursing (a euphemism for 'blessing') of father and mother to the cursing of HaShem; but with striking, it is certainly impossible — and so too the law, because the three of them are partners [in the child's creation]. \\n\\nOne may say: the entire passage [works by] 'not only this, but also this,' and thus we interpret it: not only 'one who strikes a man and he dies' alone, that he should be killed — for he killed him intentionally — but also 'who did not lie in wait' (Exod. 21:13), who killed unintentionally — he goes into exile, and if the blood-avenger reaches him outside the cities of refuge, he may kill him. And not only one who killed him and they are not standing by the altar, who are liable to death — but also 'this one who is with My altar you may take to die' (Exod. 21:14). And not only one who killed him — that they are liable to death — but also this: 'one who strikes his father and his mother' — even without [causing] death, but if he made a wound in them, he is liable to death. And not only one who strikes his father and his mother, that he is liable to death — for in any case he has wounded his fellow — but also this: 'one who steals a person' — even though he did not wound him at all, he is liable to death. And not only one who steals a person is he liable to death — for in any case he laid hands on him — but also this: one who curses his father and his mother, who did not lay a hand on them at all, is liable to death. <b>[His father and his mother]</b> — the vav in place of 'or.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:59:35.097222966Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}